#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18dc53b3-03ab-5649-8fb0-de461024a5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417787} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2bf559-c6e4-56a2-a728-3d0c5adf819f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417788} {:answer/id #uuid "3f689a2a-f4e1-53c0-8a22-5936c1c9857a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417791} {:answer/id #uuid "a71b86c9-6729-5559-b5d7-43ee35ceae47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417800} {:answer/id #uuid "a91a4cd9-65c4-5d08-a8a1-36ab03b7d971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b2bf559-c6e4-56a2-a728-3d0c5adf819f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417788} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5bc6201-b1a5-5eb7-b325-cd8c3c42ef38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e4c0564-b5b1-55bc-bc17-3dffa3a951a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158601" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16604d5a-129d-54c3-83f1-2dbdb9c13b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417786} {:answer/id #uuid "40d9eb9a-aabc-5cf9-8235-dc55fba04a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417793} {:answer/id #uuid "472664cf-4eed-5797-99be-1d353c048466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417794} {:answer/id #uuid "70e7043b-4a4b-5f86-9660-b7c6a4edac74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417797} {:answer/id #uuid "ff04d84b-3945-56d8-abdb-c06778ca1398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40d9eb9a-aabc-5cf9-8235-dc55fba04a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417793} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9aa40d2-6ab6-5496-ab09-1db4a0c06d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbe53a96-4c4c-5897-ba48-d4861ec6cb49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158547" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x-3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c94ec9c4-650f-5d39-9294-651b03b172bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Singular Value Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "766d0c5a-5abc-5b7d-9ced-05213b2666d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418110} {:answer/id #uuid "7718b520-dda1-5f3a-bc7b-0d6b3cb9898b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418111} {:answer/id #uuid "99ad27ff-65f7-5f3d-abde-578637efa421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418123} {:answer/id #uuid "ad41e705-b0fd-57cc-9d10-850ea2d27a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418125} {:answer/id #uuid "edad4670-ba38-528d-afdc-042641739a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ad27ff-65f7-5f3d-abde-578637efa421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418123} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb04f7a-e9c5-545a-b755-ad9c8bf9de09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d3ee109-94d4-53ed-8971-ac2ef97dce27" :question/math-academy-id "q-95901" :question/problem "Given the singular value decomposition $A=UΣ{V}^{T},$ where $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $Σ$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix\n- $U$ is a diagonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $m\\times m$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $Σ$ must be a $3\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix,the matrix $V$ in the singular value decomposition $UΣ{V}^{T}$ must be orthogonal $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. The matrix $U$ must be orthogonal but not necessarily diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2c0c306-a99a-558a-be4a-efd202ff8369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418751} {:answer/id #uuid "a60eb3ab-afb3-5fa3-81f8-2c88c661879b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418752} {:answer/id #uuid "b53d73b5-cdbe-5038-ac9f-f388bc73961d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418753} {:answer/id #uuid "bc85a2f4-6927-58bb-a2be-eaa0257991e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418754} {:answer/id #uuid "d7996a02-da25-5836-9723-3f3bd4f67f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7996a02-da25-5836-9723-3f3bd4f67f7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418759} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0664c98-aa8f-5458-ad72-e93d50a1082f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac83e377-f632-58f6-8bed-4004335968a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80514" :question/problem "For the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $u⊥H,\\,$ where $H={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥V,\\,$ where $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $u⊥W,\\,$ where $W={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H,V$ and $W$ as ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}},{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and ${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}}$, respectively.  \n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $u$ and the vectors in each set. \n\n- For the set $H:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {h}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-1)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {h}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1=-8\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸H.$\n\n- For the set $V:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {v}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 0=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {v}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 3+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥V.$\n\n- For the set $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =(-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 0+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=0 & ✓ \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{3} & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-2)=3\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $u⊥̸W.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d16442d-19e4-582c-8e49-f56bc5ac7989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419011} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4765c6-63f6-569c-bd76-1517cf62b38c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419012} {:answer/id #uuid "91c67dbf-ae46-5cea-83af-1664e56a1941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419013} {:answer/id #uuid "eb70dc47-8da4-592d-92b1-cbf8738afc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419014} {:answer/id #uuid "fea9db8f-ed00-5f80-923f-1ea3dcb8fe89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d16442d-19e4-582c-8e49-f56bc5ac7989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419011} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6809d48-fda2-518b-8b59-a579c69de413" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b47df0de-b5ee-5562-b36a-a659cbe48d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-125796" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n\nII. ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n\nIV. $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51ed1169-4dcb-5651-bbbd-c7b884f63478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419610} {:answer/id #uuid "5eef336e-d43f-594c-8c6c-e0250235815a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419612} {:answer/id #uuid "85e9b13d-72c1-546a-b6fc-eafc300cdb59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419617} {:answer/id #uuid "965cecae-b949-5417-b3cc-5e430c2f2b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419619} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f886d7-3b46-5e40-b8ea-797128add774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e9b13d-72c1-546a-b6fc-eafc300cdb59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419617} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9776a6-3e68-58ea-b6e5-5ee338447367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c37fbba7-25b2-5279-9899-188d8dedca8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103380" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-7≡27\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $-5≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $11≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $5∤(-7-27).$ Notice that $-7-27=-34,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $6∤(-5-15).$ Notice that $-5-15=-20,$ which is not divisible by $6.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(11-18).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-18)÷7=-7÷7=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc1a27e-8427-57e6-ac2e-b947a4b1556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419603} {:answer/id #uuid "7a197d0d-9913-5fb2-bf3c-4835cbe300af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419614} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9d3ee8-5a6d-521f-b277-71c7303fe530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419615} {:answer/id #uuid "8a94c9aa-c1e4-5350-85e8-39c118369fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419618} {:answer/id #uuid "c959a564-e622-5a0d-8e5b-fc810aa60b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bc1a27e-8427-57e6-ac2e-b947a4b1556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419603} :answer-field/id #uuid "3da5eb72-bc77-5ebc-a47a-3006549126c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9350880-d00e-5d41-adb3-52fc6c8e0846" :question/math-academy-id "q-113664" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-5≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $-23≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $-7≡-1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-5-8).$ Notice that $-5-8=-13,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $6∣(-23-(-5)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-23-(-5))÷6=-18÷6=-3\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true because $2\\,|\\,(-7-(-1)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-(-1))÷2=-6÷2=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14749e55-fcd9-5cab-b71b-ab77bde17dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419604} {:answer/id #uuid "31c492b8-531d-5421-96e8-37acf0421d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419607} {:answer/id #uuid "436c60b7-f2a7-5cc5-ada7-157322f4a843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419608} {:answer/id #uuid "98edc3ec-d794-589b-8439-5ef103206375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419620} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6cf5ba-4043-5780-8f58-4c07333d4125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98edc3ec-d794-589b-8439-5ef103206375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419620} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adb391f-ba37-5aef-9e23-79d9c1428c63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee3460a5-a9ce-5793-8247-ccd3db242273" :question/math-academy-id "q-113675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-36≡-19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $15≡-26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\n- $-27≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-36-(-19)).$ Notice that $-36-(-19)=-17,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $10∤(15-(-26)).$ Notice that $15-(-26)=41,$ which is not divisible by $10.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(-27-9).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-27-9)÷4=-36÷4=-9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12910645-377d-54ef-bc0b-422296caf8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419661} {:answer/id #uuid "2090546c-8891-5b10-af98-9204905ac08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419662} {:answer/id #uuid "27f9e9c7-23c3-5ee6-82bd-1ed602f60cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419664} {:answer/id #uuid "39e6d5c4-ef80-5978-98b7-b10c7c9e811a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419665} {:answer/id #uuid "da77cae2-0a6d-576c-9bbf-669849034a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2090546c-8891-5b10-af98-9204905ac08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419662} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b5fd09-d567-55da-9493-1ac629b8d62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e737a421-d643-51f0-9d67-b9556fcc15c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-97060" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d5b6db-f2d7-57e8-a2a5-1d92b46e0512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420031} {:answer/id #uuid "60887104-df4a-53c7-a034-7138745e6c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420036} {:answer/id #uuid "75e38757-7df1-5cf4-b8a8-25b31d2f0338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420038} {:answer/id #uuid "afaa078f-f8ba-5d53-96fa-429e2e0695f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420048} {:answer/id #uuid "e34f1333-ff13-539f-98a3-6487bacbdcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34f1333-ff13-539f-98a3-6487bacbdcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420056} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a743b7-fda2-51fe-bfdd-1a87d90bbfe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48fbd1ea-16f2-572a-98a1-c1983af51b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-221923" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $3∣(14x+21y)$\n- $7∣(14x+21y)$\n- $4∣(14x+21y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n14x+21y=7(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $7∣(14x+21y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da65dc0-01b4-5b68-9fb5-2b24392ab3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420032} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0c5418-7d81-5d69-80db-f47bba1fe715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420040} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb3c0b-8063-5f9d-b742-0b572de22bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420042} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5ebb79-57bf-59e0-84ab-0b8b6661babd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420044} {:answer/id #uuid "cfef5e04-f733-5d5c-8a6b-71ad9b16f171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfef5e04-f733-5d5c-8a6b-71ad9b16f171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420054} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c81008-53e2-57d5-9021-9e6bc5fda77a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63936aa1-e722-5014-a82a-6fe76f4b851d" :question/math-academy-id "q-221929" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $2$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $3$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $5$ divides $(6x-18y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=2(3x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $3x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=3(2x-6y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x-6y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=2$ and $y=1,$ we have $6(2)-18(1)=-6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb430d5-5241-56a2-96be-73f44b0f2114" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Transitivity of Divisibility"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d1ed1f-2a27-5962-9f76-7e68504f6b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420198} {:answer/id #uuid "92f86242-b5e9-5e13-99b0-1f387aeb933c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420220} {:answer/id #uuid "a873a377-31d6-5d14-bc3d-af2b01b12bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420223} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e70fa3-3d87-548c-a566-669bf0527f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420232} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c3abe4-7795-5469-8fa0-9b120fc335e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d1ed1f-2a27-5962-9f76-7e68504f6b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420198} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4feefa3-f467-563c-b592-67d4467afe30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24815c66-50ab-5aab-ac7b-25ddc673e132" :question/math-academy-id "q-221381" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $11∣k$ then $33∣k$\n\nII. If $33∣k$ then $11∣k$\n\nIII. If $33∣k$ then $3∣k$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a∣b\\,$ and $\\,b∣c$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $a∣c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $k=11.$ Then, $11∣11$ but $33∤11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $11∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$11∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $11∣k.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $3∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$3∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $3∣k.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25938baa-529b-5f86-828c-2801671fe4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420192} {:answer/id #uuid "3cabb55e-40d7-5b81-a1ac-1144923f9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420200} {:answer/id #uuid "550ce662-95ae-5c42-94f7-d6898e204e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420205} {:answer/id #uuid "7760ef9f-a800-540a-87c6-3414de29afe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420216} {:answer/id #uuid "df951e81-7965-5468-a3e1-c106088a53a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df951e81-7965-5468-a3e1-c106088a53a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420238} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb4bbf3-86cf-55ec-89a8-e2c06957f446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42a248b1-4b2a-5a7f-b673-589ac28da9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93649" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $10\\,|\\,21b$ then $10\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If $21\\,|\\,25b$ then $21\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If $15\\,|\\,9b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1.$ Therefore, we have $10\\,|\\,21b$ and $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1,$ which implies $10\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1.$ Therefore, we have $21\\,|\\,25b$ and $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1,$ which implies $21\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $b=5.$ Then, $15∣9\\cdot 5$ but $15∤5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c38ff4-4134-5851-a52f-d46fca4ccc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420186} {:answer/id #uuid "142f0a78-ef1b-5c40-b87c-2e4e6e998e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420190} {:answer/id #uuid "28a1ea30-a1c0-5800-8caf-4ea2604323dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420193} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd1e956-1cac-5094-a64b-65c5d077b526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420207} {:answer/id #uuid "afdca230-1900-5d05-8b30-94bc667faa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c38ff4-4134-5851-a52f-d46fca4ccc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420186} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f31e32-b105-56ba-945b-18f1eba552b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "553ed68f-8e74-5dda-a8b3-cdd3e89c31cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-93650" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,19b$ then ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If $12\\,|\\,35b$ then $12\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ then $22\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $b=19.$ Then, ${19}^{2}∣19\\cdot 19$ but ${19}^{2}∤19.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1.$ Therefore, we have $12\\,|\\,35b$ and $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1,$ which implies $12\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1.$ Therefore, we have $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ and $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1,$ which implies $22\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144822ca-c64b-5d44-988e-8d3f58310385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420191} {:answer/id #uuid "6b662c83-9382-549d-b78f-d6ae45b36298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420214} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35d862-ca5e-5f85-a3fd-da77890fb2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420225} {:answer/id #uuid "afc58b19-6e0c-5559-867b-c0befae68830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420226} {:answer/id #uuid "da7300a2-932d-5ccd-b80e-d390c021dccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7300a2-932d-5ccd-b80e-d390c021dccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420234} :answer-field/id #uuid "f237d89a-f9be-5d52-a1df-add8fdc50be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ddd359d-678b-53c8-9991-000fcf922944" :question/math-academy-id "q-93657" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $3\\,|\\,a$ and $2\\,|\\,a$ then $6\\,|\\,a$\n\nII. If $7\\,|\\,b$ and $14\\,|\\,b$ then $98\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If ${2}^{3}\\,|\\,c$ and $3\\,|\\,c$ then $24\\,|\\,c$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1$ and $2\\cdot 3=6.$ Therefore, we have $(2\\,|\\,a),(3\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1,$ which implies $(6\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=14.$ Then, $(7∣14)$ and $(14∣14),$ but $(98∤14).$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1$ and ${2}^{3}\\cdot 3=24.$ Therefore, we have $({2}^{3}\\,|\\,c),(3\\,|\\,c),$ and $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1,$ which implies $(24\\,|\\,c).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbae4e4-2423-5869-9afa-da82e1b33b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420188} {:answer/id #uuid "63d2cb0f-4fa8-5c73-8c7f-8dfd3f5884fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420211} {:answer/id #uuid "9f28d17f-a614-54b1-8f45-4295cb2a9f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420221} {:answer/id #uuid "c915309d-8abd-5446-a1f9-f241c7dc3116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420228} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1d996a-1c8d-5142-913e-181d70bfe766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbae4e4-2423-5869-9afa-da82e1b33b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420188} :answer-field/id #uuid "9800a386-864d-5b89-9fa0-d8050a82fd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d7153a0-178f-57d1-b332-d3ea8e076028" :question/math-academy-id "q-93658" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $4\\,|\\,a$ and $9\\,|\\,a$ then $36\\,|\\,a$\n\nII. If $5\\,|\\,b$ and $7\\,|\\,b$ then $35\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If ${6}^{2}\\,|\\,c$ and $2\\,|\\,c$ then $72\\,|\\,c$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(4,9)=1$ and $4\\cdot 9=36.$ Therefore, we have $(4\\,|\\,a),(9\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(4,9)=1,$ which implies $(36\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(5,7)=1$ and $5\\cdot 7=35.$ Therefore, we have $(5\\,|\\,b),(7\\,|\\,b),$ and $\\text{gcd}(5,7)=1,$ which implies $(35\\,|\\,b).$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $c=36.$ Then, $({6}^{2}∣36)$ and $(2∣36),$ but $(72∤36).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
